A||N||6||Seanh||Cold Engine problems...||||1||
Z||000000||Seanh||10-09-2001||12:25 AM||||Well, ever since my engine rebuild it has never run hot at all. I'v found that (while useing a brand new 195 stat) and with my fan on, the engine runs about 160 deg. at the dash gauge. Now, since its getting cold outside and I'm not useing my fan, its running under 160 deg. I'v replaced stats to no avail...probally 4 times, and they don't make difference. So what could the problem be? Over efficeint cooling system maybe? Bad gauge? Bad temp sensor? Whats your guess...<P>------------------<BR>Sean Heberly<BR>Silver 86 GT 4spd Auto, getting ready to install T-6 turbo.||||reg||1
Z||000001||88CoupeV6||10-09-2001||11:46 AM||||Why worry if it is running nice and cool??? Unless you have no heat inside  <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/forum/biggrin.gif"><P>------------------<BR>Josh<BR>   <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/uploads/newsig.jpg">    <BR>:D  <BR><A HREF="http://albums.photopoint.com/j/AlbumList?u=1249362" TARGET=_blank>My pics</A>||||reg||7
Z||000002||Phantom Rage||10-09-2001||12:33 PM||||Could it be that you are running rich?  Perhaps your O2 sensor went south?  Just a wild shot in the dark, good luck <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/forum/smile.gif">||||reg||7
Z||000003||Seanh||10-09-2001||09:32 PM||||I do have heat in the morning, but thats not what I'm concerned about. Running cool is bad for the engine just as overheating is, it causes premature wear. Plus, I can't afford the gas this engine consumes when its running this cold. It would probally run better at normal operating temp, since the computer won't run it so rich. I'm assuming the computer feeds off the signal of the same sensor that reads out on the gauge, right? So the computer is assuming the engine is running cold also and making the mixture too rich...I'v replaced the O2 sensor this summer, so it should still be good shouldn't it? I might try replacing it again to rule that problem out..||||reg||1
Z||000004||roadwarrior||10-09-2001||10:22 PM||||my guess would be a faulty temp sensor or a faulty thermostat.||||reg||1
Z||000005||Fallman||10-10-2001||01:13 PM||||Okay im pissed i just wrote a big long post and it didnt post to the page.  <P>Any way From my understanding the ECM has a differnt temp sensor then the gauge. The ECM's temp sensor is located on the intake manifold close to thermostat.  <P>As far as running cold and useing lots of fuel. You may have a Faulty Mass Air or O2 Sensors. These would both cause a rich mixture depending on the false reading they were giving.<P>As for a bad temp sensor this could also cause something if it was giving a false reading but not as drastically.<P>Well thats my 2cents, from my experience with my PAIN IN THE SIDE 86'/87' GT <P>Looking for 3.8L and 5spd ||||reg||1
Z||000006||William Federle||10-10-2001||01:18 PM||||If you want to know what temperature your engine is really running at, get a mechanical temperature gauge (about $9.00) and screw it in where the fan switch usually goes. Then connect a switch temporarily from the green and white wire from your fan switch cable to ground so that you can manually turn your fan on. I did this on my 1984 SE and found that the first mark on my temp gauge (about 1/4 full scale) was really about 190 deg F.<BR>||||reg||1
